  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Malaria Control in Mbale</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Malaria is the most common cause of illness and death in both children and adults in Mbale, Uganda. At the regional hospital, almost half of child inpatients are ill with malaria. When detected early, malaria can be treated with effective malaria medicines (ACTs) that are now generally available for free in public sector health facilities through grants from the Global Fund and the President&apos;s Malaria Initiative. If left too late, though, malaria soon becomes life threatening, especially for children. In these situations, children are almost always brought to a village health worker or health facility for help. The aim of this project is to make sure that &#x2018;what happens next&apos; will help them survive. Firstly, the project will build the skills of health workers and improve the quality of the services people receive. The project will ensure that children with danger signs of severe malaria are immediately referred to the right place and, once there, are tended to by staff with the necessary equipment, supplies, knowledge and skills, and these staff apply their skills to treat complicated malaria cases. Secondly, the project will work with families to make sure that when a carer is urgently advised to take a child with danger signs to a higher level health facility or hospital, they act on this referral advice. As a result, the number malaria-related deaths of children under five in health facilities will be reduced.</narrative>
  </description>
